  {"type": "split", "h2": "Why Midea is our first-call cold-climate heat pump", "image": "Installer servicing a Midea outdoor heat pump beside a house", "image_src": "/assets/img/brands/midea-heat-pump-install.webp", "image_fit": "cover", "image_credit": "Image: Midea", "html": "<p>Midea is one of the largest HVAC manufacturers in the world, and the EVOX G3 is the model they built for places like Ontario. Here's what that means in your furnace room.</p><p><strong>It heats when it's properly cold.</strong> A Midea cold climate heat pump is rated to keep heating at -30°C and holds strong capacity at -15°C, which covers the dead of a January cold snap in most of the province. Hyper Heat and Extreme Hyper Heat are Midea's names for this low-temperature heating performance.</p><p><strong>Inverter driven.</strong> The compressor speeds up and slows down to match your home's heat loss instead of blasting on and off. Longer, gentler cycles mean even temperatures, better dehumidification in July and less wear.</p><p><strong>R-454B refrigerant.</strong> This is the lower global warming potential refrigerant the industry is moving to. Buying a Midea R454B heat pump now means you're not stuck with yesterday's refrigerant for the next 15 years.</p><p><strong>It plays well with others.</strong> The EVOX G3 outdoor unit pairs with a matched Midea MAAHE or MAUHE electric air handler for an all-electric home, or with a gas furnace as a <a href='/heat-pump/hybrid-furnace-systems/'>hybrid system</a>. Either way the indoor coil is matched, which is what keeps the efficiency rating honest.</p>"},
